{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The portal vein is a large vein, about 8 cm long, that carries blood from the gut, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen to the liver. It forms behind the neck of the pancreas, at the level of the second lumbar vertebra, by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins. It then divides into right and left branches.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN47.8" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/portal-vein/portal-system-overview.webp" alt="Labelled diagram of the portal venous system showing veins from the stomach, spleen, pancreas and intestines joining to form the portal vein that enters the liver." caption="The portal venous system drains the abdominal gut, spleen and pancreas into the liver." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2138_Hepatic_Portal_Vein_System.jpg" width="960" height="620" /%}

## What is the portal vein?

The portal vein is a large vein that collects blood from four sources and delivers it to the liver:

- The abdominal part of the alimentary tract (the gut)
- The gallbladder
- The pancreas
- The spleen

In the liver the portal vein breaks up into **sinusoids** (wide, thin-walled blood channels between the liver cells). The sinusoids are drained by the hepatic veins into the inferior vena cava.

**Why "portal"?** Its main tributary, the superior mesenteric vein, begins in one set of capillaries (in the gut), and the portal vein itself ends in another set of capillaries (in the liver). A vein that lies between two capillary beds is a portal vein.

The portal vein is unusual because it is the only vein that has both **tributaries** (veins that join it) and **branches** (divisions that leave it).

## Formation and size

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/portal-vein/portal-vein-formation-pancreas.webp" alt="Diagram of the pancreas and duodenum with the splenic and superior mesenteric veins joining behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein." caption="The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas where the splenic and superior mesenteric veins meet." credit="Cerevisae, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axial_section_of_the_epigastric_region_showing_pancreas,_splenic_vein,_IVC,_aorta.jpg" width="960" height="720" /%}

- The portal vein is formed by the union of the **superior mesenteric vein** and the **splenic vein**.
- The union is **behind the neck of the pancreas**, in front of the inferior vena cava, at the level of the **second lumbar vertebra**.
- The **inferior mesenteric vein** usually drains into the splenic vein. It may sometimes drain into the confluence itself.
- Length is about 8 cm (the main portal vein ranges from 5.5 to 8 cm) and the diameter is usually about 1 cm.

## Surface marking

The portal vein is marked by a broad line that starts on the transpyloric plane 1.2 cm to the right of the median plane. The line runs upwards and to the right for about 8 cm.

## Course and relations

The vein runs upwards and a little to the right. It can be divided into three parts:

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/portal-vein/hepatoduodenal-ligament-relations.webp" alt="Labelled diagram of the hepatoduodenal ligament showing the bile duct, hepatic artery and portal vein, with the epiploic foramen and inferior vena cava behind." caption="In the hepatoduodenal ligament the portal vein is the most posterior part of the portal triad." credit="Dr. Swapnil Banerjee, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transverse_Section_of_Abdomen_-_Lesser_Sac_and_Abdominal_Ligaments.jpg" width="960" height="960" /%}

{% table %}
- **Part**
- **Position**
---
- **Infraduodenal**
- Behind the neck of the pancreas. The neck of the pancreas lies in front, and the inferior vena cava lies behind.
---
- **Retroduodenal**
- Behind the first part of the duodenum
---
- **Supraduodenal**
- In the right free margin of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament). Here it lies behind the common bile duct and the hepatic artery. The inferior vena cava lies behind it, separated by the epiploic foramen.
{% /table %}

In the lesser omentum the portal vein is surrounded by the hepatic nerve plexus and accompanied by lymphatics and some lymph nodes. It ends at the right end of the **porta hepatis** (the hilum of the liver) by dividing into right and left branches, which enter the liver.

The portal vein is the most posterior structure of the portal triad in the free margin of the lesser omentum, closely related to the common bile duct and hepatic artery.

## Tributaries

The portal system includes all veins that carry blood from the gastrointestinal tract, spleen, pancreas and gallbladder. The three main veins follow the embryonic gut divisions:

{% table %}
- **Vein**
- **Gut division**
- **Area drained**
---
- **Splenic vein**
- Foregut
- Lower end of the oesophagus, stomach, pancreas and first part of the duodenum (as well as the spleen)
---
- **Superior mesenteric vein**
- Midgut
- The whole small bowel from the second part of the duodenum, the caecum and ascending colon, and the right two-thirds of the transverse colon
---
- **Inferior mesenteric vein**
- Hindgut
- The remainder of the colon and the rectum. It usually opens into the splenic vein.
{% /table %}

Other tributaries are the **left gastric (coronary) vein**, the gastroepiploic veins and the pancreatic veins. The left gastric vein varies: it can drain into the portal vein, the splenic vein, or the junction of the two.

**Splenic vein:** it begins at the hilum of the spleen from the union of several veins. It runs to the right within the splenorenal ligament, behind the body and tail of the pancreas, and joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas. On the way it receives the short gastric veins, the left gastroepiploic veins, pancreatic veins and the inferior mesenteric vein.

**Superior mesenteric vein:** it accompanies the superior mesenteric artery and lies on the **right** side of the artery. It ends behind the neck of the pancreas by joining the splenic vein.

**Blood flow:** about 60% of portal vein blood comes from the gut and the rest from the spleen and pancreas.

## Branches and intrahepatic course

At the porta hepatis the portal vein divides into a right and a left branch.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/portal-vein/portal-vein-branches-liver-segments.webp" alt="Diagram of the liver with the portal vein dividing into right and left branches, with the eight Couinaud segments marked." caption="The right and left portal branches supply the liver segments." credit="Polygon data is generated by Database Center for Life Science(DBCLS)[2], CC BY-SA 2.1 jp, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 2.1 jp" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_04_Couinaud_classification.svg" width="960" height="541" /%}

- **Right branch:** short, only 2 to 3 cm. It usually divides into a right medial (anterior) sectoral branch, supplying segments V and VIII, and a right lateral (posterior) sectoral branch, supplying segments VI and VII.
- **Left branch:** runs transversely along the base of segment IV into the umbilical fissure. It gives branches to segments II and III, and feedback branches to segment IV. It also gives posterior branches to the left side of the caudate lobe.

**Variations:** the portal vein is fairly constant compared with the bile ducts and hepatic arteries. The standard pattern is found in up to 70% of people. The commonest variant is **trifurcation**, where the main vein divides into three: left portal, right anterior and right posterior branches. Variations matter in liver resection and in split liver or live donor transplantation.

## The portal tract and the liver's blood supply

Inside the liver, a branch of the portal vein, a branch of the hepatic artery and a bile duct run together in a **portal tract**, accompanied by nerve fibres and lymphatics. This group is often called the portal triad, but "portal tract" is the preferred term because a tract may hold more than one artery and bile duct pair.

Terminal portal venules and terminal hepatic arterioles supply the sinusoids. Blood then flows from the sinusoids into the terminal hepatic venules (central veins), then into sublobular veins, hepatic veins and finally the inferior vena cava.

**Dual blood supply:** the hepatic artery gives about 30% of the flow and 30 to 60% of the oxygen used by the liver. The portal vein gives the rest. This makes the normal liver resistant to anoxia, so ligating the portal vein does not cause hepatocellular necrosis.

**Buffer response:** if portal venous flow falls, hepatic arterial flow rises reflexly, and the reverse also holds. This keeps total hepatic blood flow steady during digestion.

## Streamline flow

Blood flow in the portal vein is slow, and it is streamlined, not mixed:

- Blood of the **superior mesenteric vein** drains mainly into the **right lobe** of the liver.
- Blood of the **splenic and inferior mesenteric veins** drains mainly into the **left lobe**.

Clinical meaning, as described in the textbooks: toxic or infective substances absorbed from the small intestine reach the right lobe, which may lead to toxic changes or an amoebic abscess there. Blood from the inferior mesenteric vein, lacking amino acids and similar nutrients, may affect the left lobe, leading to fibrosis or cirrhosis.

## Development

- The portal vein is derived from the **vitelline (omphalomesenteric) veins**. As the yolk sac regresses, its main tributaries come to arise from the intestine.
- The hepatic ends of the right and left duodenal parts of the vitelline veins form the **right and left branches** of the portal vein.
- With rotation of the gut, parts of the original vitelline veins atrophy. New channels, the **superior mesenteric and splenic veins**, join the system. The inferior mesenteric vein and other small radicles are later formations.
- In fetal life the left umbilical vein opens into the left portal vein. After birth the umbilical vein is obliterated, and the normal adult portal circulation is established soon afterwards. Its remnant is the **ligamentum teres**.
- The **ductus venosus** joined the left branch of the portal vein to the inferior vena cava. Its remnant is the **ligamentum venosum**. If it persists (patent ductus venosus), it may be accompanied by other congenital anomalies.

## Portosystemic anastomoses

Normally portal blood passes through the liver and drains by the hepatic veins into the inferior vena cava. This is the direct route. Smaller communications between the portal and systemic veins exist. They are small normally, but they open up when the direct route is blocked.

{% table %}
- **Site**
- **Portal side**
- **Systemic side**
- **Clinical result when portal pressure is high**
---
- **Lower end of oesophagus**
- Oesophageal branches of the left gastric vein
- Oesophageal veins draining to the azygos system
- **Oesophageal varices**, which may rupture and cause haematemesis
---
- **Umbilicus**
- Paraumbilical veins along the ligamentum teres, joining the left branch of the portal vein
- Superficial veins of the anterior abdominal wall (superior and inferior epigastric veins)
- **Caput medusae**
---
- **Anal canal**
- Superior rectal vein (continues up as the inferior mesenteric vein)
- Middle and inferior rectal veins (to internal iliac and internal pudendal veins)
- **Haemorrhoids**, which may bleed
---
- **Retroperitoneum**
- Veins of the duodenum, pancreas, ascending and descending colon, and liver
- Renal, lumbar and phrenic veins
- Collaterals that make abdominal surgery hazardous
{% /table %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Three classic sites:** lower end of the oesophagus, around the umbilicus, and the anal canal. The matching signs are oesophageal varices, caput medusae and haemorrhoids.
{% /callout %}

## Portal hypertension

**Portal hypertension** means raised pressure in the portal system. It results from many disease processes that increase resistance to portal venous flow.

- **Normal pressure:** the portal vein pressure is about 5 to 15 mm Hg. A pressure difference between the wedged hepatic vein and the inferior vena cava is normally 4 to 8 mm Hg, and higher values indicate portal hypertension.
- **Classification of causes:** blockage may be prehepatic, intrahepatic or posthepatic. Examples are portal vein thrombosis, compression of the portal vein by a tumour, cirrhosis of the liver (where the vascular bed of the liver is obliterated), and posthepatic causes such as blockage of the inferior vena cava, heart failure and Budd-Chiari syndrome.

**Effects of portal hypertension:**

- Congestive splenomegaly
- Ascites. It develops partly from portal hypertension but mainly from liver cell failure.
- Collateral circulation through the portosystemic communications (varices, caput medusae, haemorrhoids)

**Clinical points:**

- A soft liver suggests extrahepatic portal vein obstruction. A firm liver suggests cirrhosis (intrahepatic portal hypertension). A small, contracted, fibrotic liver is found when portal pressure is very high.
- **Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy** is the most reliable investigation. A barium swallow shows varices as filling defects in the lower third of the oesophagus (the "bag of worms" appearance).
- **Ultrasound with Doppler** can show splenomegaly, collaterals and reversed flow in the portal vein (hepatofugal flow). A portal vein diameter above 13 mm is a sensitive but nonspecific sign.
- Bleeding is the major complication. About 20% of patients with an episode of variceal bleeding die within 6 weeks, so it is a serious result of chronic liver disease.

**Treatment in brief:**

- Bleeding oesophageal varices: endoscopic band ligation is preferred, with injection sclerotherapy if banding is difficult.
- Bleeding gastric varices: usually injected with cyanoacrylate glue.
- Early vasoactive drugs (terlipressin, or somatostatin or octreotide) are given as supplements to endoscopic therapy.
- After the bleed is controlled, non-selective beta blockers are given to prevent rebleeding.
- Shunt operations join one of the main portal channels (splenic, superior mesenteric or portal vein) to the inferior vena cava or left renal vein. Radiologically placed transjugular portosystemic stent shunts (TIPSS) are also used.

## Portal vein thrombosis

**Portal vein thrombosis (PVT)** is a clot in the portal vein. In adults it is the main cause of **extrahepatic portal vein obstruction**.

- **Causes:** often an underlying cause such as cirrhosis, infection, inflammation (for example pancreatitis), tumour, trauma, a clotting disorder or surgery. A thrombosed portal vein that stays enlarged or grows is suspicious for an underlying tumour.
- **Acute PVT:** abdominal pain, or complications such as bowel infarction and ascites. Early anticoagulation gives a good outcome.
- **Chronic PVT:** if the clot does not recanalise, the lumen of the portal vein closes. Tortuous collateral vessels bypass the blocked part and together form a **portal cavernoma** (cavernous transformation).
- **Features of cavernoma:** it is often silent. It may cause bleeding from varices and an enlarged spleen with low blood cell counts (hypersplenism). Liver tests are typically normal, because there is little liver dysfunction.
- In patients without liver disease, PVT is usually a benign disease. The outcome depends mostly on the underlying condition and on whether the clot extends into the superior mesenteric vein.

## Surgical note

The portal vein runs close to the inferior vena cava. Portal vein injuries are first controlled by direct manual pressure. Repair or reconstruction is done in most cases, and ligation is kept for patients in extremis who would otherwise bleed to death.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein and not directly into the confluence." %}
The portal vein is formed by the union of:
- A. Superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric veins
- B. Superior mesenteric and splenic veins
- C. Splenic and left gastric veins
- D. Inferior mesenteric and left gastric veins
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The superior mesenteric vein begins in capillaries in the gut and the portal vein ends in capillaries (sinusoids) in the liver. A vein that lies between two capillary beds is a portal vein." %}
A vein is called a portal vein because it:
- A. Enters the liver at the porta hepatis
- B. Carries oxygenated blood to the liver
- C. Begins and ends in capillary beds
- D. Drains directly into the inferior vena cava
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein. It may occasionally open into the confluence of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins." %}
The inferior mesenteric vein usually opens into the:
- A. Inferior vena cava
- B. Portal vein
- C. Superior mesenteric vein
- D. Splenic vein
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The paraumbilical veins run along the ligamentum teres and connect the left branch of the portal vein with the superficial veins of the anterior abdominal wall. When portal pressure rises they open up and produce caput medusae." %}
Caput medusae develops in portal hypertension because the left branch of the portal vein communicates, through the paraumbilical veins, with:
- A. Superficial veins of the anterior abdominal wall
- B. Azygos veins
- C. Middle rectal veins
- D. Renal veins
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Blood flow in the portal vein is streamlined. Blood from the superior mesenteric vein goes mainly to the right lobe, and blood from the splenic and inferior mesenteric veins goes mainly to the left lobe." %}
Because of streamline flow in the portal vein, infective material absorbed from the small intestine reaches mainly the:
- A. Left lobe, through the splenic vein
- B. Left lobe, through the inferior mesenteric vein
- C. Right lobe, through the superior mesenteric vein
- D. Caudate lobe, through the left gastric vein
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The liver has a dual blood supply from the portal vein and the hepatic artery. This makes the normal liver resistant to anoxia, so ligating the portal vein does not cause hepatocellular necrosis." %}
Ligation of the portal vein in a normal liver does not usually cause hepatocellular necrosis mainly because:
- A. The hepatic veins reverse their flow
- B. The hepatic artery provides a second blood supply
- C. The liver needs very little oxygen
- D. Portosystemic anastomoses supply the sinusoids
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Describe the formation of the portal vein." %}
It is formed behind the neck of the pancreas, at the level of the second lumbar vertebra, by the union of the superior mesenteric vein and the splenic vein. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the course and parts of the portal vein." %}
It is about 8 cm long and runs upwards and a little to the right. It is infraduodenal (behind the neck of the pancreas), retroduodenal (behind the first part of the duodenum) and supraduodenal (in the right free margin of the lesser omentum). It ends at the porta hepatis by dividing into right and left branches.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the relations of the portal vein in the lesser omentum?" %}
It lies behind the common bile duct and the hepatic artery. The inferior vena cava is behind it, separated by the epiploic foramen. It is surrounded by the hepatic nerve plexus and accompanied by lymphatics and lymph nodes.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What areas do the three main tributaries drain?" %}
The splenic vein drains the foregut (lower oesophagus, stomach, pancreas, first part of the duodenum) and the spleen. The superior mesenteric vein drains the midgut. The inferior mesenteric vein drains the hindgut: the remainder of the colon and the rectum.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is streamline flow in the portal vein?" %}
Blood flow is slow and does not mix. Superior mesenteric vein blood goes mainly to the right lobe of the liver. Splenic and inferior mesenteric vein blood goes mainly to the left lobe.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the sites of portosystemic anastomosis and what happens in portal hypertension." %}
The lower end of the oesophagus (oesophageal varices), the umbilicus (caput medusae) and the anal canal (haemorrhoids). There are also retroperitoneal anastomoses. They open up when the direct route through the liver is blocked.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is a portal cavernoma?" %}
After a portal vein thrombosis that does not recanalise, the lumen closes and tortuous collateral vessels bypass the blocked part. Together these collaterals form the cavernoma. It may be silent or cause variceal bleeding.
{% /viva %}

